 ### Dual Marching Cubes Problematic Config Packing
-A variant focused on the memory layout of the ambiguous configuration table. It packs four consecutive 8-bit direction codes from the `problematicConfigs` array into a single 32-bit `uint` entry in the `ProblematicConfigs` array (size 64 uints for 256 bytes) using bitwise shifts and OR operations to reduce memory footprint [S4, unofficial].
+A variant focused on the memory layout of the ambiguous configuration table. It packs four consecutive 8-bit direction codes from the `problematicConfigs` array into a single 32-bit `uint` entry in the `ProblematicConfigs` array (size 64 uints for 256 bytes) using bitwise shifts and OR operations to reduce memory footprint [S3, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
 *   **Time Complexity:** $O(V)$ where $V$ is the number of voxels in the bounding box, as it performs a single pass with constant-time bitmask updates and table lookups [S3, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Depends on the volume size and mesh output. The `ProblematicConfigs` table is small (256 bytes or 64 uints) [S4, unofficial]. The `DualPointsList` is 512 uints [S4, unofficial]. The output mesh size scales with the surface area.
-*   **Measured Performance:** Tested on a 36x36x36 grid (derived from 64^3 volume with boundary reduction) [S4, unofficial]. Theoretical worst-case triangle buffer size is $34^3 \times 18 = 707,472$ triangles [S4, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Guarantees a manifold mesh by resolving C16/C19 ambiguities [S4, unofficial]. Efficient table-based lookup for ambiguous cases [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires precomputation of the problematic config table [S2, unofficial]. Boundary handling requires careful neighbor checks to avoid out-of-bounds access [S4, unofficial]. In flat regions with coarse adaptive octrees, dual partitioning may fail to generate interior samples, leading to topological holes [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** When a watertight, manifold mesh is required from a uniform voxel grid and standard Dual Marching Cubes produces non-manifold artifacts.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Depends on the volume size and mesh output. The `ProblematicConfigs` table is small (256 bytes or 64 uints) [S3, unofficial]. The `DualPointsList` is 512 uints [S3, unofficial]. The output mesh size scales with the surface area [S3, unofficial].
+*   **Measured Performance:** Tested on a 36x36x36 grid (derived from 64^3 volume with boundary reduction) [S3, unofficial]. Theoretical worst-case triangle buffer size is $34^3 \times 18 = 707,472$ triangles [S3, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Guarantees a manifold mesh by resolving C16/C19 ambiguities [S3, unofficial]. Efficient table-based lookup for ambiguous cases [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Requires precomputation of the problematic config table [S2, unofficial]. Boundary handling requires careful neighbor checks to avoid out-of-bounds access [S3, unofficial]. In flat regions with coarse adaptive octrees, dual partitioning may fail to generate interior samples, leading to topological holes [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When to use:** When a watertight, manifold mesh is required from a uniform voxel grid and standard Dual Marching Cubes produces non-manifold artifacts [S3, unofficial].
 *   **When not to use:** When feature preservation is more important than manifold topology (use Hermite DualMC) or when the grid is highly adaptive and coarse in flat regions (use Primal methods) [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Manifold Dual Marching Cubes was developed to address the topological inconsistencies in Dual Marching Cubes, specifically the ambiguous configurations C16 and C19 identified in Nielsen's work [S2, unofficial]. The method relies on precomputing a lookup table that maps these problematic configurations to their ambiguous face direction, allowing for neighbor-based resolution [S2, unofficial]. It is used in GPU-based implementations for real-time surface extraction [S4, unofficial]. The underlying dual marching cubes approach was introduced by Sweldens and Schröder [SW04] (cited in S1), and the manifold resolution technique is detailed in works by Wodniok [S2, unofficial] and others [S4, unofficial].
